The Docker (or Podman) socket can be read and written by anyone on the
system: every local user, and every service account a compromised
service runs as, can drive the container engine, and through it is
root on the host.

To fix it, 'chmod 660' the socket and keep it owned by root and the
docker group, then find what changed it: a SocketMode= in a drop-in for
docker.socket, a -G or --group option, or a script run at boot.
